# CineSync OS M1 Workflow Overview

## Purpose

CineSync OS M1 is a proof-of-concept workflow for AI-assisted filmmaking. It demonstrates how a written script can be transformed into structured scene planning, shot planning, camera controls, and video generation.

The system is designed as a director-facing workflow rather than a simple prompt box.

## Workflow Summary

```text
Script → Scenes → Shots → Camera Controls → Generation Request → Video Preview
```

## Step 1: Script Input

The user begins by entering or pasting a script into the Script Column.

The script may include:

- Scene descriptions
- Character actions
- Emotional beats
- Dialogue or narration
- Location and mood references

The purpose of this stage is to capture creative intent in a flexible format.

## Step 2: Script-to-Scene Parsing

The script is converted into scene-level blocks.

Each scene can contain:

- Scene title or label
- Location or setting
- Action summary
- Mood or tone
- Key visual elements

This makes the original script easier to plan, review, and convert into shots.

## Step 3: Scene Selection

The user selects a scene from the generated scene list.

The selected scene becomes the active creative unit for shot planning.

## Step 4: Shot Generation

For the selected scene, the system generates multiple shot suggestions.

A shot may include:

- Shot description
- Subject focus
- Camera intent
- Visual framing direction
- Movement idea
- Cinematic purpose

The goal is to help the user move from story-level thinking to production-level planning.

## Step 5: Camera Engineering Controls

The user can adjust basic cinematography controls before generation.

M1 includes controls such as:

- Lens selection
- Camera movement
- Framing choice

These controls help convert a generic visual idea into a more directable shot request.

## Step 6: Prompt / Request Preparation

The selected scene, selected shot, and selected camera controls are combined into a generation-ready request.

In the public repo, this layer should be described at a high level only. The exact private prompt structure and advanced orchestration logic should remain private.

## Step 7: Video Generation

The generation layer sends the prepared request to a video-generation provider or model integration.

In M1, this validates the end-to-end flow from script to generated output.

## Step 8: Preview and Download

The generated video is displayed in the Generation Column.

The user can:

- Preview the result
- Review output quality
- Download the result if available
- Iterate on shot or camera inputs

## M1 User Journey

```text
1. Paste script
2. Generate scenes
3. Select scene
4. Generate shot list
5. Choose shot
6. Select lens / movement / framing
7. Generate video
8. Preview result
```

## Why This Workflow Matters

Most AI video tools start with a blank prompt box. CineSync M1 explores a more structured direction:

- The script remains the source of truth.
- Scenes create structure.
- Shots create production planning.
- Camera controls make generation more directable.
- The user remains involved at every stage.

## M1 Boundaries

M1 is intentionally basic and focused on proving workflow feasibility.

It does not claim to solve the full AI filmmaking problem.

M1 validates:

- Workflow structure
- UI interaction model
- Basic AI-assisted planning
- Generation pipeline connection

Future layers can improve:

- Character consistency
- Style locking
- Scene continuity
- Multi-shot editing
- Reference-based generation
- Local or cloud model routing

## Public Repo Positioning

The public version should position CineSync M1 as:

> An AI-native filmmaking workflow prototype that converts script intent into structured scene planning, shot planning, camera controls, and video generation.

Avoid positioning it as:

- A finished production system
- A full AI video platform
- A complete replacement for directors or VFX teams

## Recommended Demo Flow

For recruiters, collaborators, or reviewers, demonstrate the project in this order:

1. Show the script input.
2. Generate scenes.
3. Select one scene.
4. Generate shot suggestions.
5. Select one shot.
6. Adjust lens, movement, and framing.
7. Generate or show a sample video output.
8. Explain how the architecture can support stronger models later.

## Key Takeaway

CineSync M1 proves the core idea:

> AI video generation becomes more useful when it is guided by a structured filmmaking workflow instead of isolated prompts.
